---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MortgageCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/MortgageCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mortgage-calculator">
  <MortgageCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use this mortgage calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Enter the <strong>home price</strong> and your <strong>down payment</strong>. The toggle
        switches between a percentage and a cash amount, and the other figure shows underneath.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the <strong>interest rate</strong> and <strong>term</strong>. Fifteen and thirty years
        are the common US fixed terms; twenty-five is standard in the UK and Canada.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fill in the escrow items: <strong>property tax</strong> (as a rate or an annual amount),
        <strong>home insurance</strong>, <strong>HOA or condo fees</strong> and the
        <strong>PMI rate</strong> if your down payment is under 20%.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add an <strong>extra monthly principal payment</strong> to see how much interest you save and
        how many months come off the term.
      </li>
      <li>
        Scroll to the schedule for a payment-by-payment breakdown, switch between the monthly and
        yearly views, and export it as CSV.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What is in a monthly mortgage payment</h2>
    <p>
      Lenders call it PITI (principal, interest, taxes and insurance), and most borrowers have two more
      line items.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Principal is the part of the payment that reduces the debt. It starts small and grows each
        month.
      </li>
      <li>
        Interest is the lender's charge on the balance you still owe. It starts large and gets smaller
        each month.
      </li>
      <li>
        Property tax is set by your local authority, usually as a percentage of the assessed value. It
        is collected monthly into an escrow account and paid once or twice a year for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        Homeowners insurance is required by the lender for the life of the loan, and is escrowed the
        same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        PMI (private mortgage insurance) protects the lender, not you. It is charged while your
        loan-to-value ratio is above 80%, usually 0.3% to 1.5% of the original loan amount per year.
      </li>
      <li>
        HOA or condo fees are not part of the mortgage, but you pay them each month and lenders count
        them when they assess affordability.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The formula behind the payment</h2>
    <p>
      The level monthly payment on a fixed-rate loan comes from the standard annuity formula:
      <code>M = L × i / (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code>, where <code>L</code> is the loan amount,
      <code>i</code> is the monthly interest rate (the annual rate divided by twelve) and
      <code>n</code> is the total number of payments. The mortgage calculator shows this formula with
      your own numbers in the formula panel.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each month, interest is charged on the remaining balance, and the rest of the payment reduces the
      principal. So the split changes a lot over the life of the loan. On a 30-year loan at 6.5%, the
      first payment is about 86% interest, and principal becomes larger than interest only at about
      year eighteen.
    </p>

    <h2>Monthly principal and interest per 100,000 borrowed</h2>
    <p>
      Multiply the figure for your rate and term by your loan amount in hundreds of thousands. A
      400,000 loan at 6.5% over 30 years is 4 × 632.07 = 2,528.28 a month before tax and insurance.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Interest rate</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">15 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">20 years</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">30 years</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>3.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">690.58</td><td style="text-align:right">554.60</td><td style="text-align:right">421.60</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">714.88</td><td style="text-align:right">579.96</td><td style="text-align:right">449.04</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">739.69</td><td style="text-align:right">605.98</td><td style="text-align:right">477.42</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">764.99</td><td style="text-align:right">632.65</td><td style="text-align:right">506.69</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">790.79</td><td style="text-align:right">659.96</td><td style="text-align:right">536.82</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">817.08</td><td style="text-align:right">687.89</td><td style="text-align:right">567.79</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">843.86</td><td style="text-align:right">716.43</td><td style="text-align:right">599.55</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">871.11</td><td style="text-align:right">745.57</td><td style="text-align:right">632.07</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">898.83</td><td style="text-align:right">775.30</td><td style="text-align:right">665.30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7.5%</td><td style="text-align:right">927.01</td><td style="text-align:right">805.59</td><td style="text-align:right">699.21</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8.0%</td><td style="text-align:right">955.65</td><td style="text-align:right">836.44</td><td style="text-align:right">733.76</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What paying extra saves</h2>
    <p>
      On a 360,000 loan at 6.5% over 30 years, the scheduled payment is 2,275.44 and the total interest
      is 459,164, more than the house. A fixed extra amount on each payment changes that a lot, because
      each extra dollar of principal removes all the future interest that dollar would have generated.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Extra per month</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Paid off in</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Time saved</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Interest saved</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>0</td><td style="text-align:right">30 yr 0 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">0</td><td style="text-align:right">0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td style="text-align:right">26 yr 7 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">3 yr 5 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">62,910</td></tr>
          <tr><td>250</td><td style="text-align:right">22 yr 10 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">7 yr 2 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">127,719</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500</td><td style="text-align:right">18 yr 9 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">11 yr 3 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">196,133</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td style="text-align:right">14 yr 0 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">16 yr 0 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">270,608</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Before you overpay, check two things: that your loan has no prepayment penalty, and that the
      lender applies the payment to principal and does not hold it as a prepaid instalment. Many
      lenders require you to tell them explicitly.
    </p>

    <h2>How accurate is this?</h2>
    <p>
      The amortisation is exact. Payments use arbitrary-precision decimal arithmetic and round to the
      cent as a servicer does, and the final instalment takes the rounding difference. So the schedule
      matches a lender statement to the penny for a standard fixed-rate loan.
    </p>
    <p>
      The escrow items are estimates. Property tax and insurance stay flat in today's money, but both
      usually rise each year, and a reassessment after purchase is common in the US. PMI is a fixed
      percentage of the original loan amount and stops at 80% loan-to-value against the purchase price.
      Your lender may use an appraised value, and FHA loans have different rules where mortgage
      insurance can last the life of the loan. Closing costs, discount points, escrow shortfalls and
      mortgage interest tax relief are not included.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing you type leaves your device</h2>
    <p>
      A few kilobytes of JavaScript do the arithmetic locally, so your price, savings and payoff plans
      stay on your device. There is no lead form and no rate-quote broker behind the results.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
